// Tauride Garden data: the DESIGN VALUES of the bridges, the dam and the reeds (docs/TAURIDE-REMAKE.md, phase T4b). Hand-authored (NOT generated by tools/tauride/build.mjs), pure data: no imports.
// The mapped facts (the eight bridge ways, the six sites, the deck endpoints, the dam position) are in data/bridges.js; the water datums (the water level, the beds, the bank, the collider contour) in data/ponds.js.
// Nothing here is surveyed: every number is a design value of the plan (docs/TAURIDE-REMAKE.md section 7: "deck width 3 m, stair, bridge and gate details") or of this phase, named with its reason.
// Frame: metres, y = 0 is the street level. The one place where the bridge kit (bridges.js), the stairs of the alleys (paths-draped.js: a stair at a bridge head rises to the deck) and the tests read their numbers.

/** How a bridge end reaches dry land (the deck extension of the old garden.js, bridges.js extendToDry): a deck end moves outward along its axis in `step` m steps until the shore distance there is at least
 *  the collider contour (data/ponds.js COLLIDER.contourY, 0.5 .. 0.8 m inland of the waterline) + `margin` m, at most `max` m. The data's ends lie on the water outline's dry side but five of the twelve stand ON the contour. */
export const EXTEND = { step: 0.25, margin: 0.6, max: 8 };

/** The deck meets the alley: the approach starts at the ground + `headLift` m = the offset of the draped ribbons (paths-draped.js PATHS_DRAPED.offset 0.03), so the deck surface continues the ribbon surface without a step. */
export const HEAD_LIFT = 0.03;

/** The slope the approaches of a deck may have (rise per metre: 1:4, the plan's walking rule; the harness asserts it along the route); a ramp is lengthened until it holds. */
export const MAX_RAMP_SLOPE = 0.22;

/** Walkers (people/nav.js surfaceY through the deck service's deckLift): the feet stand this much above deckY on a garden bridge (the deck IS the walking surface; the Paradny podium keeps the engine's pavement lift 0.12). */
export const WALK_LIFT = 0.02;

/** the three types of the kit (data/bridges.js BRIDGE_SITES[].type) and the dam. Heights are relative to the ground at the approach ends (+ HEAD_LIFT), spans to the water crossing of the axis. */
export const TYPES = {
  /** No.3 and No.4: a shallow arched iron deck between granite abutments at the shore, approach ramps on the banks, marble terminal posts at the abutments, cast-iron balustrade, iron arch ribs under the deck */
  'iron-arch': { headRise: 0.35, crown: 0.40, abutmentFace: 0.4, abutmentLen: 2.0, deckThick: 0.22, railH: 1.05, ribs: 4, ribDrop: 0.35, postMarble: 1.2, color: { deck: '#4a4d50', rail: '#2f3234', rib: '#3a3d40', abutment: '#8b8a84', post: '#d9d6cc', ramp: '#c9a98a' } },
  /** No.1: the humped wooden bridge, planks by colour, wooden rails, the T3 stairs at both ends rise `headRise` to the deck heads (docs/TAURIDE-REMAKE.md 7.1: the 4 + 4 steps say the deck ends stand 0.6 m above the lawn) */
  'wood-humped': { headRise: 0.6, crown: 0.45, deckThick: 0.18, railH: 1.0, plank: 0.5, color: { deck: '#8a6446', deck2: '#7a583c', rail: '#6b4a30', beam: '#5e3f28', abutment: '#8b8a84' } },
  /** No.2 and No.5: a flat metal footbridge on granite abutments, short ramps at the ends, simple rails */
  'metal-flat': { headRise: 0.30, rampMin: 2.0, abutmentFace: 0.4, abutmentLen: 1.6, deckThick: 0.16, railH: 1.0, color: { deck: '#5a5e63', rail: '#3a3d40', abutment: '#8b8a84', post: '#d9d6cc' } },
  /** the dam: a solid granite body across the channel, standing proud of the water on both faces up to the walkway on its crest, a coping (the overflow sill) above the deck line on the spill face (the Small Pond, north: fix round 2), iron rails (see DAM below).
   *  Fix round 1: the body and the coping are a warm light granite, the walkway a dark paving: the stone mass reads against the deck and the rails (the GPU QA saw a flat grey slab like No.2 / No.5 and no stone at all) */
  weir: { deckThick: 0.0, railH: 1.0, color: { body: '#a39b8e', coping: '#b7b0a3', deck: '#6b6a66', rail: '#3a3d40', abutment: '#a39b8e' } },
};

/** per-site overrides of the type values (the Big Bridge No.3: the chain of a 6.7 m compacted ramp, the 9.4 m iron span and a 5.7 m ramp stands higher over the water than the Small Bridge: five arches, span 13.15 m) */
export const SITE_OVERRIDES = { 'No.3': { headRise: 0.65, crown: 0.35, ribs: 5 } };

/** the rails (every type): posts every `postSpacing` m, a top rail at railH and a mid rail; the COLLIDER of a rail (invisible): a box `colliderThick` m thick whose inner face stands `colliderIn` m inside the deck edge,
 *  from `colliderBelow` m under the deck to `colliderAbove` m over it. physics.js skips a box when feet + STEP_HEIGHT (0.45) is at or above its top: a hop from the deck (apex 0.73) has feet + 0.45 = 1.18 < 1.3, so the rail holds. */
export const RAIL = { postSpacing: 1.5, postW: 0.08, railW: 0.07, midH: 0.55, colliderThick: 0.5, colliderIn: 0.0, colliderAbove: 1.3, colliderBelow: 1.0 };

/** the walking surface of a deck for the player (core/physics.js addWalkRamp): one ramp per `rampLen` m of the axis, `rampThick` m thick, `widthInset` m narrower than the deck on each side: NEGATIVE = wider, the ramp runs on UNDER the rail
 *  colliders (a 5 cm slit between the ramp edge and the rail let a walker drop from the stair of the wooden bridge onto the bank below the deck, inside the opened shore ring: the deck ring of tauride-height-test found it); the shore colliders
 *  of ponds.js are cut away inside the deck rectangle grown by `pad` m, so the rails (0.5 m thick, from the deck edge outward) overlap the cut chord ends */
export const WALK = { rampLen: 1.0, rampThick: 0.6, widthInset: -0.15, pad: 0.45, sample: 0.25 };

/** the dam (data/bridges.js DAM: the authoritative position; the walkway way w112880645): `crest` is the walkway level = the street level at its east end (the garden outline passes 0.3 m east of the way end: the dam's east abutment is the street kerb; data/ponds.js QUAY.top is the same 0).
 *  ONE water level (data/ponds.js WATER_Y: the T4a decision, the union is one polygon): the overflow is a VISUAL. Fix round 1 (the GPU QA: "a flat dark-grey footbridge slab, no granite mass visible, the sheet a flat pale plank, the foam a pale rectangle"):
 *  the stone BODY rises from `bodyBelowBed` m under the bed to the crest (the walkway slab sits IN it: 0.45 m of granite stands above the water on both faces) and runs `bodyOut` m beyond the deck on the UPSTREAM face;
 *  on the SPILL face a granite COPING `copingW` m wide stands `copingRise` m ABOVE the deck line (the sill: visible from the deck and from the water it falls into); the falling water is a NAPPE hanging from the coping lip
 *  down to the water in two rows bulging `sheet.bulge` m out from the face, one translucent pale tone (`sheet.color`: the granite shows through) with a brighter thread `sheet.streakW` m wide per metre (`sheet.streak`);
 *  the foam on the water at its foot is `foamW` m wide with a WAVY far edge (`foam.jitter`: the share by which the width varies between the metre marks). The decal material carries one opacity (`opacity`).
 *  Fix round 2 (the lens: the sill was built on the north face and DOCUMENTED as 'the lake (north) face', but in the data the lake is SOUTH of the dam): the faces come from the data now. The lake (the Big Pond, `data/bridges.js DAM.mouthOnLake`
 *  (1273.8, -1655.1), the end of the channel canal w112853212 on the lake outline) lies on the +z = SOUTH side of the dam axis; the north side is the Small Pond (`data/ponds.js SMALL_POND`: the bed BED_SMALL north of mouthZ -1655.3,
 *  the dam at z -1674). `spillToLake` says which face carries the sill and the nappe: false = the face AWAY from the lake (the Small Pond, north): the water came up the Ligovsky canal into the Big Pond (the lake, upstream) and left
 *  through the Small Pond down the Samoroyka to the Neva (north), so the overflow dam of the 1790s spilled from the lake INTO the Small Pond; the geometry of fix round 1 already stood on that face, only its name was wrong.
 *  true would put the sill and the nappe on the lake (south) face and the bodyOut on the north face. The GPU QA's 'lake face' poses (dam2-lakeface-*, from the south, yaw 0) see the plain body; its 'north face' poses see the sill. */
export const DAM = { crest: 0.0, spillToLake: false, bodyBelowBed: 0.3, bodyOut: 0.25, copingRise: 0.15, copingW: 0.8, foamW: 0.9, sheet: { bulge: 0.16, streakW: 0.12, color: '#d3e4e3', streak: '#f4f9fa', opacity: 0.65 }, foam: { color: '#e6efee', jitter: 0.45 } };

/** the budgets of the kit (tools/tauride/bridge-test.mjs): triangles of the six sites together (plan C6: ~10 k), draws (the bridges, the dam water), colliders */
export const BUDGET = { triangles: 10000, draws: 2, colliders: 320 };

/** The reed belts (reeds.js): instanced clumps on the shore band. `band`: shore distance range [water side, land side] in m (negative = in the water); `belt`: the belts along the shore come from a slow noise of the arc length
 *  (`period` m, a belt where the noise exceeds `threshold`); `spacing` m between clumps inside a belt (jittered); `count` the target instance count; keep-outs in m: alley ends (`wayEnd`), any alley (`way`), the bridge decks (`deck`, from the extended deck rectangle), the dam axis (`dam`),
 *  the memorial descents (`steps`), the garden outline (`outline`: the quay and the skirt stretches: no bank there). The clump: `blades` triangles of `height` m (random in the range), vertex colours base -> tip. */
export const REEDS = { band: [-0.55, 0.35], belt: { period: 23, threshold: 0.64 }, spacing: 0.9, count: 500, keepOut: { wayEnd: 3.5, way: 2.2, deck: 3.0, dam: 5.0, steps: 4.0, outline: 3.0 }, blades: 7, height: [1.1, 1.9], lean: 0.22, color: { base: '#4f6b2a', tip: '#a8b35a', dry: '#b9a35e' }, draws: 1 };
